-- Leak guard for the per-call SIMD vector temp-box leak.
--
-- `go` is self-tail-recursive with a vector parameter, so llvm_toplevel gives
-- that parameter a NATIVE <4 x float> TCO slot and the entry prologue unboxes
-- the incoming argument once per call. Nothing released that box: one
-- 32-byte march_simd_alloc cell leaked per INVOCATION (not per iteration),
-- unbounded over a long-lived process.
--
-- The fix is caller-side: the call site releases the box IT created, but only
-- for callees listed in ctx.native_vec_params -- callees whose entry prologue
-- provably does one GEP+load and never retains the pointer. The complementary
-- canary for the shapes that must NOT be released is
-- test/native/simd_vector_escape_arg.march.
--
-- SIGNAL: live_allocs(), NOT RSS -- same rationale, and same conversion, as
-- native_arr_fold_leak_probe.march. march_simd_alloc cells are March heap
-- objects, so live_allocs() (the runtime's always-on live-object gauge,
-- incremented in march_alloc/decremented on every free-on-rc-zero path)
-- counts a leaked box directly, with no allocator free-list behaviour, no
-- page-granularity rounding, and no OS/platform baseline to calibrate away.
-- The ORIGINAL guard here asserted an absolute max-RSS threshold (< 32 MB),
-- calibrated on Darwin (healthy ~2.7 MB, leaked ~67 MB). It went RED the
-- first time CI's ubuntu leg ran it (PR #269 removed this probe's prior
-- Darwin-only gate), at max RSS 128,761,856 B -- not a leak, just Linux's
-- ~122 MB process baseline that the macOS-derived band never accounted for.
-- An absolute-RSS threshold is not portable; a live-object count needs no
-- calibration at all.
--
-- Numbers, measured on this fixture (Darwin arm64, --opt 2, 2,000,000 calls):
--   caller-side release present   live_allocs delta =         1
--   release deleted (sabotage)    live_allocs delta = 2,000,001
-- The one leftover object is incidental runtime/stdlib state, not a
-- per-call leak; deleting the release adds exactly 2,000,000 -- one leaked
-- march_simd_alloc cell per invocation of `go`, the defect stated exactly
-- rather than estimated.
--
-- The probe prints its live_allocs delta to STDERR via IO.warn as the final
-- line, so stdout stays diffable against .expected while the dune rule reads
-- the delta out of the captured stderr file -- same three-rule structure as
-- native_arr_fold_leak_probe (run / stdout diff / threshold assertion).
--
-- .expected is NOT interpreter-produced for STDOUT either (2,000,000
-- iterations do not finish under the tree-walker in any reasonable time;
-- same posture as simd_bounds_panic, which is compiled-only by design). The
-- stdout value is arithmetic: 2,000,000 calls x sum of splat(1.0) over 4
-- lanes = 8000000. (live_allocs() itself returns a constant 0 in the
-- INTERPRETER -- see eval.ml -- which is exactly why the delta goes to
-- stderr: the stdout .expected must stay identical between the two
-- backends, and it does, since it never depends on live_allocs().)
--
-- specs/progress/2026-08-11-simd-tco-entry-box-leak.md
mod SimdLeakProbe do
  needs IO.Console

  pfn go(v, i : Int) : Float do
    if i <= 0 do Simd.sum_f32x4(v)
    else go(Simd.add_f32x4(v, Simd.splat_f32x4(0.0)), i - 1) end
  end

  pfn drive(n : Int, acc : Float) : Float do
    if n <= 0 do acc
    else drive(n - 1, acc +. go(Simd.splat_f32x4(1.0), 1)) end
  end

  fn main(_cap_console : Cap(IO.Console)) do
    let before = live_allocs()
    println(drive(2000000, 0.0))
    let after = live_allocs()
    IO.warn(int_to_string(after - before))
  end
end
